uct to market so  you know the FDA has the ability to have  provoked drug drug makers test drugs  before they go on the market to see if  they're safe the UK has a financial  services regulatory sandbox that would  allow financial services creators to do  the same thing to kind of test market  something before it has to go through  all of those regulatory hurdles so those  are three things I think that regulators  could do to help speed up solving this  problem yeah so the CFPB as you know  does have a project catalyst that is  helping innovators bring new products to  market how do you think the regulators  need to change in terms of interacting  with new providers that may be offering  more competition to a market that often  sorely lacks it  well I think kind of opening up the  regulations and being a little bit more  open to it is the first thing and to  realize to have conversations with these  providers and work with them more  closely to make a determination about  whether those products are safe versus  harmful before just kind of seeing that  they don't fit into the pre-existing box  I think regulators are often out of  habit of the mindset of kind of viewing  products that are coming that  pre-existing institutions like banks and  now we have a lot of entrepreneurs who  are coming from all different sectors  that are creating products and systems  not always products that have the  potential to help people but because  they don't fit into one of those  pre-existing boxes they kind of don't  make it through the screen it's  interesting my experience as a regulator  is every time regulators try to work  with entrepreneurs and innovators the  banks are the ones who really get upset  about it because those are the  technologies that really could disrupt  their business models that's right well  that's interesting that you say that you  know some of the people that I spoke  with who are the innovators when I said  you know why aren't the banks doing this  instead of you guys partly what they  said was that these are little they're  almost like little laboratories that are  funded with relatively small amounts of  money they're very nimble sometimes the  people who had them up have had  experience in the banking industry and  in the policy agnus policy makers and  you know what they say is the banks  don't really they're not as dynamic and  nimble and able to talk across silos as  these newer FinTech organizations are  and that you know they kind of rather  have these experiments going on at the  micro level and then the banks will just  buy them if they you know say since  there's a future for them so that's a  possibility too that banks will innovate  not by growing these things organically  but by purchasing other firms that's  right  yeah now tell me a you spend some time  in the book talking about Millennials  and the millennial experience with  banking ones that they will not be going  into a branch but they certainly  have maybe day-to-day contact with  payment apps or other banking services  how do you see that changing the  business of banking yeah good question I  mean first of all Millennials I think  get a bad rap when it comes to money  they actually are saving at higher rates  than the previous generation did and  they're pretty on top of it  significantly higher free nificantly  higher rates yes so we think of them as  being this kind of irresponsible  generation that's simply relying on  their parents to fund what they do but  they're actually being pretty savvy they  have a lot of student debt for example  and so they have no choice but to really  think about how they're going to deal  with that they are the largest  generation even larger than baby boomers  25% of our population so no matter what  we say about them they will change the  face of consumer financial services and  the four biggest banks make they're all  make their top ten list of least liked  kind of most hated brands so they are  not big in