e millions of  who are considered subprime many of them  moved to prime and super prime so if  that credit scoring model was instituted  on a much larger scale we'd see millions  and millions of people who would have  access to cheaper better credit right so  those two things those innovations are  kind of like these systems that run  underneath the whole system but that  would really open up the financial  markets and existing financial services  to a much wider group of people but  there's certainly some losers from those  innovations  there's certainly industries that depend  on slower deposits clearing in people's  bank accounts there's certainly as you  mentioned companies that make quite a  bit of money sending money overseas will  those companies die or will they simply  figure out a way to stop those  innovations from moving forward it's  hard to say you know I don't know the  ins and outs of Western Union as well as  probably I should but I think it's  likely that companies would want to take  on the innovation and compete right what  happens now for example is that you know  if I go to Western Union or a check  casher to send money to Guatemala all I  really see is my money going across the  counter and a pretty hefty fee but the  money will stop several times between  here and my cousin in Guatemala let's  just say and every time it stops it  takes a little time and it costs a  little money and there's a potential for  error  and that the folks who work in this  industry call that friction so what the  ripple folks are doing is trying to take  that friction out of the system we found  the surface that would be good for  Western Union to it because it will be  able to compete with other providers how  about this evening exactly there may be  some winnowing in those industries but  that's creative destruction you know  that's that's how industry always works  and I think in this case the benefits to  the consumer make it reasonable to  follow those innovations so let's talk  about some of the other solutions that  you think are important  other than fostering innovation you  actually embrace some of the  pushes do we increase the Postal  Service's participation in the financial  services marketplace and tell us more  about that sure well I think it's a  start off I would say that we've had  we've had a range of relationships when  we think of the relationship between the  banking sector and the public sector if  we go back to Supreme Court justice  Louis Brandeis and Thomas Jefferson and  Woodrow Wilson they all argued that the  banking system was kind of like the  railroads you know it was sort of a  public utility it was a different  structure a different kind of it was a  part of the infrastructure mate that  makes the economy run and so that and  banks get particular kinds of benefits  from government like the FDIC's  insurance which you know after the crash  of 29 we instituted this insurance that  created a system so that if you put your  money in a bank you wouldn't lose it if  the banks went bust so that's a big  advantage to the banks and so the  argument is that banks should do  something in return for that insurance  and other kinds of benefits they get  favorable treatment on loans from the  government etc what's happened really  since the 80s when we entered a big long  period of deregulation is that that  relationship has kind of moved from  banks being responsible to a larger  public interest to banks being more  responsible to their own profitability  and efficiency and government enabling  that so that's all context to say that I  think we need to move that relationship  back more to decide where banks have to  do more in the public interest and I  think there are a few ways to do that  the postal banking service system that's  been proposed off and on by many people  is one way to do that in which post  offices the postal system would create  bank accounts for people and provide  financial services and that was done for  a period mostly for immigrants earlier  in the century got kind of phased out  but the argument is that the postal  service would not be in the business of  making a huge profit from it but rather  providing these services as part of what  it does now the last time that  was raised as as an idea was four or  five years ago and it was a white paper  coming from the inspector general's  office of the Postal Service and that  paper argued that it would be a benefit  to consumers and also that the Postal  Service could make a profit which I  don't think is really feasible nor  should be the desired goal I think we  should consider a public banking system  through the Postal Service or not but  that the reason to do it would be simply  be that it's the right thing to do that  that it should be a right of every  citizen to have safe affordable access  to financial services other things that  I think we could do along those lines  one is that we have a program right now  called the Lifeline program that gives  all Americans access to telephone  service the argument and I believe it  was created under Reagan was that you  know you need a phone line in order to  call for one one or 911 call phone to  participate in the job market and so if  you can't afford a phone will subsidize  the phone service providers to make it  available to you well we could do that  same logic to think about banks - given  the amount of money that's being made  it's hard to argue that they should be  subsidized to do this but but it's also  doesn't fit their business model they're  not necessarily making a profit on these  people so that's something I think that  could be considered as well so I  definitely think we need to pursue more  of these ideas that get government more  involved in financial services although  in the current administration I don't  think that's a likely prospect so what's  the likelihood do you think of advancing  what you call in the book financial  justice what do you think is the roadmap  for that in these very uncertain times  yeah well I think the first thing that  we need to do is to really think about  what financial justice means are all  Americans being treated the same way by  financial institutions and in other ways  that are a little bit maybe outside of  the the actual financial services system  but very much linked up with it and  those kinds of things I would liken to  what happened in Ferguson where we saw  Ferguson Missouri where we saw African  Americans important  neighborhoods being unfairly targeted  for things like broken taillights and  minor infractions that led them to  basically fund the municipal system debt  collection practices that target certain  groups over others so I think the first  step and this is something I'm starting  to work on myself is to kind of collect  all of that information and lift it up  to show that as we sit right now that  the fact that there is a huge amount of  financial injustice in terms of certain  groups and individuals being targeted in  different ways and paying more and then  I think the next thing is to really kind  of change the way that we're talking  about this issue right now as I  mentioned before we we still use these  categories of unbanked banked and  underbanked which I think also have a  kind of deficiency built into them you  know it sounds like you're unhealthy or  you're uneducated or there's something  wrong with you exactly if we change the  conversation to be one that focuses on  financial health which is something the  Center for financial services innovation  has really pioneered then we get to a  different conversation we start asking  what do what do Americans need in order  to be able to budget and plan to save  for retirement to deal with the shock to  their income these things that have  become increasingly common but that  Americans are less and less able to deal  with and I think that's the beginning of  the roadmap to then start to argue for  policies that enable financial health  you know defining the problem is one of  unbanked  given the way that banks are working  with lower and moderate income people  and this new middle class it doesn't  make sense as a solution so we have to  redefine the problem well professor  serve and I just want to thank you for  being here today the book again is the  unbanked  it's available in bookstores and online  and we hope you all take a look at it  thanks so much thank you

