welcome to book TV my name is Rohit  Chopra and today I'll be talking to  professor Lisa serve on a professor of  City and regional planning at the  University of Pennsylvania and author of  the un-- banking of America how the new  middle class survives which was just  released this month Lisa thank you for  being here thanks for having me it's  great to be here so let's just start why  did you write this book and who is it  actually written for it's a great  question I wrote the book in a nutshell  because I could not understand  originally why if alternative financial  services like check cashers and payday  lenders were so bad for people why so  many people were using them and in the  course of my research which took a few  years I learned that banks were not  really serving the low below and  increasingly the middle class and I also  realized that there were some good  alternatives coming on board and so I  kind of wanted to tell that whole story  it's the first book I've written that's  not an academic book meaning it's really  accessible for a wider audience and so I  wrote it for policy makers for people  who are working in the financial  services industry and also for people  like you and me who may or may not be  happy with the consumer financial  services that they're currently using  great so let's talk a little bit about  what you mean by alternative financial  services providers select level-set yeah  on what's that and what why do you call  it alternative yeah that's a good  question  so I see the consumer financial services  industry as being broken into three  components one would I'd call a  mainstream component which is banks and  credit unions for the most part the  second is alternative check cashers  payday lenders pawnshops rent-to-own  shops there's a whole set of them and  they're growing as consumer financial  services changes and technology grows  and then the third is informal financial  services which are not really on the map  of policymakers so much but they're used  by many many Americans and so you talked  a little bit about how people think some  of these alternative  providers are quote unquote bad yeah and  say more about why why you think that  that's the impression and it may be true  but put a finer point yeah sure so you  know when I started doing this work I  was reading about consumer financial  services and the way that many policy  makers had framed the issue  the FDIC primarily the Federal Deposit  Insurance Corporation which has put out  every two years now for several years  something called the survey of banked  and unbanked households and they were  kind of looking they had kind of created  this framework of banks unbanked and  underbanked banked being households that  use a bank account and do not use  alternative financial services unbanked  being those people who have no bank  account at all and under banks being  people who have a bank account but also  rely on alternative financial services  and the message the message of those  policy reports really was that everyone  should use a bank account the latest  research says that about 20% of  Americans are under banked another 8%  are unbanked have no bank account at all  and the implicit message was banks are  definitely best for people and those who  traffic in on the alternative financial  services are predatory they're sleazy  they're part of the poverty industry  they're just out to take advantage of  people and you know that's when I got  back to this original question or what  led me to the question that made me say  you know what if so many people are  using these services there must be a  reason besides the fact that they're  ignorant or don't know any better  because I've done work in low-income  communities for 20 years for my whole  career and I know that families who have  very little money know where every penny  goes and so it struck me that there had  to be a better reason for why people  were using these service