out it I think right about how they  are doing things that are that are  responsible in terms of their money and  I think the other thing that we have to  do is with all of the stuff that we've  been talking about over the last however  many minutes is to think about how the  condition of the American worker has  really changed and Millennials are  really coming maybe the first generation  to really face a very different reality  and one of the things that really struck  me when I did focus groups and  interviews with people that age was how  they did not relate to the American  Dream as it was kind of put in front of  me and my parents of you know being able  to get a good job that came with a  stable predictable income job one job  per person maybe even one job per  household although that's not the way it  wasn't my house buy a home save for  retirement save your kids college mean  my parents were both teachers not very  fancy jobs but they were stable and they  allowed them to do all of those things  I've talked to lots of people who have  master's degrees and said you know if I  take the job of my dreams I might not be  able to have children or I definitely  won't be able to MIT to buy a house and  so we're still kind of projecting that  American dream as though it's a reality  but they're kind of waking up as they  graduate from college or graduate school  and saying I don't think I can I don't  think that's a reality and that  sometimes they feel like they've fallen  short we have this huge problem I think  of shame and embarrassment and personal  failing around money and sometimes  they're resentful or just resigned by  saying you know my parents had this but  it's not going to be for me so I don't  know if that really changes the larger  public's perception but I think they've  gotten kind of a raw deal and we look at  only maybe the fact that they're moving  back home or not gainfully employed and  judge them for that without looking at  this larger context which frankly is the  same context that has created an upsurge  in use of payday loans and check cashers  and in some kind of some sense is it  living at home maybe the more  financially responsible thing to do for  men you that's right I mean I'm glad I  did not have to go back home so I don't  think we should just assume that yeah  these young people are so happy to go  back and move in with mom and dad and  have their laundry done and their meals  cook for them you know if they're also  feeling like they can't grow up and kind  of go through these stages of adulthood  and it's because of this financial  context and do you think it will make  Millennials more skeptical of taking on  debt especially after their student debt  experience I think it will I mean you  know I definitely talked to some people  for whom their debt was already so big  that they felt like you know what's a  little bit more but I would say on the  whole many of them are feeling like yeah  they're very reticent to run up their  credit cards or to you know take on debt  to buy a house or something because  they're not so sure that they can rely  on that income you know one of the most  disturbing aspects of my interviews with  Millennials was how many people talked  about not being able to do things like  go to their friends weddings or go home  for holidays which seemed like luxuries  maybe but I thought about it in terms of  their social networks you know that  these as we we as Americans don't always  live close to where we grow up and you  depend on these kinds of life events  in order to maintain that that web of  support and they often had to opt out of  those things because they just in order  to be financially responsible they  couldn't participate but then they felt  that those ties were weakening and I  think from the perspective of civil  society that that's a troubling thing  yeah I heard from one gentleman who had  a student loan who and he defaulted and  his grandmother was a cosigner and his  relatives did not really want him to  come home for Thanksgiving because his  grandmother was being financially harmed  by it so I think it does impact people's  psyche as well when they default yeah I  think it absolutely does and I also  talked to many parents who did not  expect to have to take in their adult  children and were squeezed by those  obligations and kind of felt like the  parents were taking out loans that they  shouldn't have taken on because they  wanted to help their children and and  first you know felt guilty as parents do  even when things aren't their fault so  there are really strong  intergenerational dynamics happening  here but the upside of more Millennials  in the financial services marketplace is  that there may be more opportunities for  innovation and disruption absolutely so  you spend a good amount of time talking  about some of the companies you've  already mentioned but what do you see do  you see these thin tech providers or  innovators do you think any of them  really has a chance to fundamentally  transform the business of banking in the  open marketplace I think I think they do  and I would say I would just amend your  question a little bit to say financial  services not just banking right so I'll  talk about two that I think are not  about products and services but that can  change kind of the infrastructure of  banking and credit one of them I'm one  of them I write about in the book is  called ripple which is located in San  Francisco where a lot of the FinTech  happens ripple is a company of startup  that is looking to create what they call  an internet of value kind of like the  Internet of information that we all use  to send  texts around all the time it would  create a system that would enable money  to move from one place to another  without cost and immediately the same  way it happens if I send you an email  right so what that does if that catches  on and it looks like it's got a pretty  good chance of doing it is it makes it  possible for me to deposit that check in  my account and have it available to me  immediately  now I don't have to wait the three or  four days for it to clear in my account  or if I want to remit money to my  relatives in Guatemala or send money  across the country those things are  rather expensive if you go to a Western  Union or a check casher so that right  there eliminates a huge barrier to  people getting bank accounts right  because they can get their money when  they need it so that's one innovation  that has you know it has to do almost  with the plumbing of the banking system  but not the products and services itself  another one is around credit scoring I  looked at a firm called L to see that  was recently bought by trans Union which  is one of the three big credit bureaus  and what they've done is pilot a new  credit scoring formula so you know if I  were to get my credit score done right  now it would kind of look at a snapshot  of me today how much money I'm using at  the credit I have available and whether  I've been late on things and what my  bank says about whether it overdrafted  or not this other new credit scoring  model looks at a thirty day period so  it's a broader period of time not just a  snapshot which is like liable to be more  fair and it also looks at all kinds of  other assets and liabilities that are  kind of on my personal balance sheet  including did I pay my rent and my  utilities things that your typical  credit score doesn't have does not  include exactly and what mike monde le  the founder of LTC found is that first  off there are millions of people in the  United States who have no credit score  which means if they try to get a loan  they're going to pay a lot of money for  it that  because they don't have the information  that traditional credit bureaus use well  if you apply this different credit  scoring model they can all be scored and  many of them get prime scores if you  rescore everyone the millions of  who are considered subprime many of them  moved to prime and super prime so if  that credit scoring model was instituted  on a much larger scale we'd see millions  and millions of people who would have  access to cheaper better credit right so  those two things those innovations are  kind of like these systems that run  underneath the whole system but that  would really open up the financial  markets and existing financial services  to a much wider group of people but  there's certainly some losers from those  innovations  there's certainly industries that depend  on slower deposits clearing in people's  bank accounts there's certainly as you  mentioned companies that make quite a  bit of money sending money overseas will  those companies die or will they simply  figure out a way to stop those  innovations from moving forward it's  hard to say you know I don't know the  ins and outs of Western Union as well as  probably I should but I think it's  likely that companies would want to take  on the innovation and compete right what  happens now for example is that you know  if I go to Western Union or a check  casher to send money to Guatemala all I  really see is my money going across the  counter and a pretty hefty fee but the  money will stop several times between  here and my cousin in Guatemala let's  just say and every time it stops it  takes a little time and it costs a  little money and there's a potential for  error  and that the folks who work in this  industry call that friction so what the  ripple folks are doing is trying to take  that friction out of the system we found  the surface that would be good for  Western Union to it because it will be  able to compete with other providers how  about this evening exactly there may be  some winnowing in those industries but  that's creative destruction you know  that's that's how industry always works  and I think in this case the benefits to  the consumer make it reasonable to  follow those innovations so let's talk  about some of the other solutions that  you think are important  other than fostering innovation you  actually embrace some of the  pushes do we increase the Postal  Service's participation in the financial  services marketplace and tell us more  about that sure well I think it's a  start off I would say that we've had  we've had a range of relationships when  we think of the relationship between the  banking sector and the public sector if  we go back to Supreme Court justice  Louis Brandeis and Thomas Jefferson and  Woodrow Wilson they all argued that the  banking system was kind of like the  railroads you know it was sort of a  public utility it was a different  structure a different kind of it was a  part of the infrastructure mate that  makes the economy run and so that and  banks get particular kinds of benefits  from government like the FDIC's  insurance which you know after the crash  of 29 we instituted this insurance that  created a system so that if you put your  money in a bank you wouldn't lose it if  the banks went bust so that's a big  advantage to the banks and so the  argument is that banks should do  something in return for that insurance  and other kinds of benefits they get  favorable treatment on loans from the  government etc what's happened really  since the 80s when we entered a big long  period of deregulation is that that  relationship has kind of moved from  banks being responsible to a larger  public interest to banks being more  responsible to their own profitability  and efficiency and government enabling  that so that's all context to say that I  think we need to move that relationship  back more to decide where banks have to  do more in the public interest and I  think there are a few ways to do that  the postal banking service system that's  been proposed off and on by many people  is one way to do that in which post  offices the postal system would create  bank accounts for people and provide  financial services and that was done for  a period mostly for immigrants earlier  in the century got kind of phased out  but the argument is that the postal  service would not be in the business of  making a huge profit from it but rather  providing these services as part of what  it does now the last time that  was raised as as an idea was four or  five years ago and it was a white paper  coming from the inspector general's  office of the Postal Service and that  paper argued that it would be a benefit  to consumers and also that the Postal  Service could make a profit which I  don't think is really feasible nor  should be the desired goal I think we  should consider a public banking system  through the Postal Service or not but  that the reason to do it would be simply  be that it's the right thing to do that  that it should be a right of every  citizen to have safe affordable access  to financial services other things that  I think we could do along those lines  one is that we have a program right now  called the Lifeline program that gives  all Americans access to telephone  service the argument and I believe it  was created under Reagan was that you  know you need a phone line in order to  call for one one or 911 call phone to  participate in the job market and so if  you can't afford a phone will subsidize  the phone service providers to make it  available to you well we could do that  same logic to think about banks - given  the amount of money that's being made  it's hard to argue that they should be  subsidized to do this but but it's also  doesn't fit their business model they're  not necessarily making a profit on these  people so that's something I think that  could be considered as well so I  definitely think we need to pursue more  of these ideas that get government more  involved in financial services although  in the current administration I don't  think that's a likely prospect so what's  the likelihood do you think of advancing  what you call in the book financial  justice what do you think is the roadmap  for that in these very uncertain times  yeah well I think the first thing that  we need to do is to really think about  what financial justice means are all  Americans being treated the same way by  financial institutions and in other ways  that are a little bit maybe outside of  the the actual financial services system  but very much linked up with it and  those kinds of things I would liken to  what happened in Ferguson where we saw  Ferguson Missouri where we saw African  Americans important  neighborhoods being unfairly targeted  for things like broken taillights and  minor infractions that led them to  basically fund the municipal system debt  collection practices that target certain  groups over others so I think the first  step and this is something I'm starting  to work on myself is to kind of collect  all of that information and lift it up  to show that as we sit right now that  the fact that there is a huge amount of  financial injustice in terms of certain  groups and individuals being targeted in  different ways and paying more and then  I think the next thing is to really kind  of change the way that we're talking  about this issue right now as I  mentioned before we we still use these  categories of unbanked banked and  underbanked which I think also have a  kind of deficiency built into them you  know it sounds like you're unhealthy or  you're uneducated or there's something  wrong with you exactly if we change the  conversation to be one that focuses on  financial health which is something the  Center for financial services innovation  has really pioneered then we get to a  different conversation we start asking  what do what do Americans need in order  to be able to budget and plan to save  for retirement to deal with the shock to  their income these things that have  become increasingly common but that  Americans are less and less able to deal  with and I think that's the beginning of  the roadmap to then start to argue for  policies that enable financial health  you know defining the problem is one of  unbanked  given the way that banks are working  with lower and moderate income people  and this new middle class it doesn't  make sense as a solution so we have to  redefine the problem well professor  serve and I just want to thank you for  being here today the book again is the  unbanked  it's available in bookstores and online  and we hope you all take a look at it  thanks so much thank you
 

